<h2>A fourteen-year-old claim and a limitation attack</h2>
<p>The contractor&rsquo;s suit, filed in 2014, sought recovery under a construction contract of 2000, with running bills spanning 2000 to 2003. On its face, the claim was a decade out of time, and the society attacked the plaint under Order VII Rule 11 CPC. The trial court sustained the plaint, treating the society&rsquo;s balance-sheet entries for 2004 to 2013 as acknowledgments under Section 18 of the Limitation Act. The Delhi High Court reversed in revision, finding no acknowledgment after 2005. The Supreme Court restored the trial court&rsquo;s view — and the suit.</p>

<h2>The doctrine, consolidated</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. Entries can acknowledge.</strong> <em>Asset Reconstruction Co. v. Bishal Jaiswal</em> settled — in the IBC context — that balance-sheet entries can constitute acknowledgment under Section 18. <em>Axis Bank v. Naren Sheth</em> carried the principle forward. This decision applies it squarely to an ordinary civil recovery suit.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. Read the whole document.</strong> A balance sheet is not a bare schedule of figures. The notes annexed and the auditor&rsquo;s report form part of it, and the acknowledgment inquiry must read them together. Here the 2004-05 balance sheet and bank reconciliation referenced cheques issued to the plaintiff.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. Continuity matters.</strong> The liability was carried forward year after year to 2013 — indicating, in the Court&rsquo;s phrase, a &ldquo;subsisting jural relationship&rdquo; of debtor and creditor through the limitation-critical period.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. The threshold is prima facie.</strong> At the Order VII Rule 11 stage the court tests only whether the plaint discloses a case within limitation. Whether the entries truly match the quantum claimed is for evidence at trial.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why the stage of proceedings decided the outcome</h2>
<p>The High Court had, in substance, conducted a mini-trial in revision — parsing which post-2005 entries did or did not identify the plaintiff&rsquo;s debt. The Supreme Court&rsquo;s correction is as much about procedure as limitation: plaint rejection is a demurrer-stage remedy confined to the plaint and its documents, and where those documents include accounts that can plausibly be read as acknowledgments, the plaintiff is entitled to a trial on that reading. Defendants retain every limitation defence at trial; what they cannot have is a summary burial of a claim their own books kept alive.</p>

<h2>Practice implications on both sides of a recovery suit</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For claimants.</strong> Before writing off an old receivable, obtain the debtor&rsquo;s filed financial statements — from the Registrar of Cooperative Societies, the ROC, or disclosure — and search for the liability. A carried-forward entry can resurrect a claim thought dead.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For debtors.</strong> Accounts are admissions. A liability shown year after year to satisfy auditors will be read against the entity in court. Disputed claims should be disclosed as contingent and disputed, in terms, if the limitation defence is to be preserved.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For drafters of plaints.</strong> Plead the acknowledgments specifically — document, date, entry — and annex them. The prima facie case must appear from the plaint&rsquo;s own papers to survive Order VII Rule 11.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For auditors&rsquo; clients.</strong> The notes and reconciliations annexed to the balance sheet are part of the acknowledgment analysis. Precision there is not accounting pedantry; it is limitation strategy.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">Section 18 requires the acknowledgment to be made before the expiry of the existing limitation period, in writing and signed — conditions satisfied, prima facie, by successive signed financial statements each made while the previous period still ran. The chain, once broken, cannot be re-welded; hence the significance of the year-on-year carry-forward in this case.</div>
